+# Decision: Concurrent Visit Edits Use Whole-Visit Revisions
+
+## Context
+
+Field workers correct the same visit independently after comparing notes, and tablets can be offline for 48 hours. When two complete submissions derive from the same starting visit state, the record store must decide how they become the visit's current record. The policy determines the submission envelope, what review work exists, which visits can enter the partner export, and what tablet-sync must retain locally. At stake is a direct trade: more coordinator review, or automatic combination that may join observations the server cannot establish as scientifically compatible.
+
+The HLD requires preserving every submitted edit, supporting offline capture, keeping the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable, and letting two coordinators operate without a dedicated systems administrator. It also excludes automated botanical validation or inference of scientific intent. The record store cannot infer every relationship between fields; a species identification and its cover estimate are an example of edits that are scientifically related without being the same field.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+- **Scientific-record coherence — major.** The current record must remain a coherent observation set. Automatic combination must not join edits whose scientific relationship the server cannot establish. This engages the HLD non-goal on inferring scientific intent and the tenet *Do not silently discard a submitted edit*.
+- **Inspectability of the current value — major.** A coordinator must be able to trace the current record to the submitted observations that produced it. This engages the tenet *Keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable*.
+- **Review workload — major.** The number of visits requiring coordinator attention before the Friday export must remain operable by two coordinators.
+- **Operational burden — major.** The mechanism must fit a team with no dedicated systems administrator. Merge rules that require ongoing design, testing, and debugging are a standing operational cost. This engages the tenet *Prefer workflows two coordinators can operate without a dedicated systems administrator*.
+- **Failure behavior — major.** When the mechanism misjudges whether edits are related, it must fail toward review rather than toward a silently combined record.
+- **Offline submission cost — moderate.** The bytes a tablet must send after a 48-hour disconnection affect retry time and data use.
+- **Tablet-side state complexity — moderate.** The local state and transform logic required to prepare a submission affect what tablet-sync must store and retry.
+- **Export readiness — moderate.** The proportion of visits that can enter the Friday CSV without coordinator work affects whether the export deadline is met.
+- **Evolution cost — moderate.** Adding or changing visit fields must not require a new family of merge behaviour.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Whole-visit revisions
+
+Every accepted submission is a complete visit payload. A submission names the revision it started from. A revision based on the current revision becomes current; any other revision becomes pending. A pending revision is competing review work. A coordinator resolves the visit by selecting one pending or current revision and declining the others. The current record is always exactly one submitted payload, never a composition.
+
+Demonstration: revision R1 is the first submission; R2, based on R1, becomes current after an observer-name correction; R3, also based on R1, adds a missing species and becomes pending. Review compares the whole R2 and R3 records. Selecting R3 declines R2, and the visit becomes export-eligible.
+
+- **Scientific-record coherence:** strong. Each candidate is a complete record submitted by a worker; the server never joins fields from different submissions.
+- **Inspectability:** strong. The current revision is one submitted payload, and the resolution records the selected revision and contributing revision IDs.
+- **Review workload:** weak. Every submission that missed the current revision creates review work, including edits to unrelated fields.
+- **Operational burden:** strong. There is no merge-rule catalogue to maintain; coordinators compare whole records and select one.
+- **Failure behavior:** strong. The worst case is an extra review, not a silently combined record.
+- **Offline submission cost:** partial. The whole payload is sent on every submission; at visit scale this is small, but it is larger than a change set.
+- **Tablet-side state complexity:** strong. Tablet-sync retains a base revision ID and submits a whole payload.
+- **Export readiness:** weak. Unresolved visits are held out of the CSV, so a larger review queue can delay export.
+- **Evolution cost:** strong. New fields need no new merge rules.
+
+### Field-aware three-way merge
+
+A submission carries the base state the worker started from and a structured set of changes. The server applies changes to disjoint fields automatically and sends overlapping changes to review. Maintaining the policy requires rules for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries. Tablet-sync retains base snapshots and sends structured changes. Review-export needs field-level provenance and a review view for overlapping changes, because the current record may be composed from multiple submissions.
+
+Demonstration: one worker corrects an observer name while another adds a missing species. A base snapshot plus structured changes lets the server combine both edits without review because they touch different fields. If both workers edit the same field, the overlap goes to a coordinator. However, if one worker changes a species identification and another changes that species' cover estimate, the fields are disjoint and the rules may combine them without noticing that the observations are scientifically related.
+
+- **Scientific-record coherence:** weak. Field identity does not encode scientific relatedness; disjoint edits can still be related in ways the rules do not understand.
+- **Inspectability:** partial. Field-level provenance can trace each value to a submission, but the current record is composite and its shape depends on merge rules. Coordinators need a provenance interface to understand it.
+- **Review workload:** strong. Disjoint changes combine automatically, reducing coordinator work.
+- **Operational burden:** weak. Scalar, observer-list, and species-entry rules need ongoing design, testing, and debugging; someone must own rule behaviour.
+- **Failure behavior:** weak. A rule gap can silently combine scientifically related edits, and the error may reach export unless provenance review catches it.
+- **Offline submission cost:** strong. A base snapshot reference plus structured changes is smaller than a whole payload.
+- **Tablet-side state complexity:** partial. Tablet-sync must retain base snapshots, build structured changes, and handle provenance for retries; this is more local state and transform logic.
+- **Export readiness:** strong. More visits resolve without coordinator action, which helps the Friday deadline.
+- **Evolution cost:** weak. Each new field or list shape needs merge rules and tests.
+
+## Selection
+
+Whole-visit revisions win on scientific-record coherence, inspectability, operational burden, failure behavior, and evolution cost. Field-aware merge wins on review workload, offline submission cost, and export readiness. The decision accepts the larger review queue to avoid maintaining merge rules that can combine scientifically related edits without noticing. It turns on the HLD tenets *Do not silently discard a submitted edit*, *Keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable*, and *Prefer workflows two coordinators can operate without a dedicated systems administrator*, together with the non-goal on inferring scientific intent.
+
+Committing to whole-visit revisions forecloses automatic combination of concurrent edits: every submission that misses the current revision becomes review work. It obligates tablet-sync to retain each visit's current revision ID, submit whole payloads, distinguish current from pending outcomes, and refresh current state after an idempotent replay. It obligates review-export to present whole-revision comparisons, hold unresolved visits out of the CSV, and resolve by selecting one revision and echoing the reviewed set. Neither downstream node may introduce field-level merge semantics.
+
+Field-aware three-way merge remains the credible alternative. It is the only option that reduces review volume and increases the number of visits ready for export without coordinator action. It becomes the better choice if the review queue proves untenable for two coordinators, if Friday exports are delayed by unresolved visits, or if merge behaviour can be restricted to fields whose edits have no scientific relationship to other fields. Those conditions would reopen the trade rather than invalidate this decision.
