+A revision's status is one of:
 
-## Downstream Contract Work
+- `current` — the visit's current revision.
+- `superseded` — was current, was replaced by a revision based directly on it, and remains on the current lineage.
+- `pending` — accepted, not on the current lineage, and not yet declined.
+- `declined` — considered in a resolution and not selected.
 
-After selection, define the submission envelope, current-state response, unresolved-work representation, and resolution operation here. Tablet-sync will size its local storage and retry state machine around that envelope. Review-export will size its coordinator interface and export eligibility rules around the unresolved-work representation. Both must link back to this node rather than invent their own concurrency semantics.
+Selecting a pending revision makes the previous current revision and every other pending revision `declined`. Revisions already marked `superseded` stay `superseded`. A revision in a resolution's reviewed set is `current` or `pending`, never `superseded` or `declined`.
+
+## Submission
+
+A submission carries the visit ID, base revision ID, idempotency key, and complete visit payload. The server authenticates the author and, for a key not seen before, validates that:
+
+- The visit ID is a UUID.
+- The payload contains its required fields with valid types: plot reference, observation date, observer list, and species cover estimates. `notes` is optional; an absent optional field and a null optional field are equivalent.
+- The plot reference resolves in the catalog and matches the visit's fixed plot ID. An archived plot UUID remains valid for historical visits.
+- The base revision ID belongs to the same visit; null is valid only when the visit has no revisions at acceptance.
+- An unknown or cross-visit base revision ID is rejected.
+
+The response reports the visit ID, the new revision ID, the revision's status at acceptance (`current` or `pending`), the visit's current revision ID at acceptance, whether the visit became unresolved, and the server receipt time.
+
+Idempotency keys are scoped to the authenticated author. A key is evaluated before current-state validation. A repeated key whose visit ID, base revision ID, and canonical payload are identical returns the original acceptance result verbatim, including the original status and current revision ID; it is not a current-state read, so the caller must refresh current state. A repeated key with any different field is rejected and leaves the original submission unchanged. Canonical payload comparison sorts object keys, preserves array order, and treats absent and null optional fields as equal.
+
+## Retrieval
+
+Current-state retrieval requires an authenticated worker and returns:
+
+- The visit ID.
+- The current revision: revision ID, base revision ID, payload, author ID, and receipt time.
+- An `unresolved` flag.
+- The pending revision IDs, empty when the visit is resolved.
+
+Current state is returned even when the visit is unresolved. Callers must not treat a provisional current revision as export-eligible. Retrieving a visit ID that has never had an accepted revision returns not found.
+
+Revision-history retrieval requires the coordinator role and returns every revision for the visit ordered by acceptance sequence, each with its status, base revision ID, payload, author ID, and receipt time. It also returns the visit's resolution outcomes: coordinator ID, selected revision, contributing revision IDs, and resolution time. This is enough for a coordinator to compare whole revisions side by side and for an auditor to see why the current revision stands.
+
+Unresolved-work retrieval requires the coordinator role and returns one item per unresolved visit. Each item contains the visit ID, the current revision, the pending revisions with full payloads and metadata, and the exact reviewed-revision set the coordinator must echo to resolve the visit.
+
+## Resolution
+
+A resolution request carries an idempotency key scoped to the coordinator, the visit ID, the selected revision ID, and the exact reviewed-revision set from retrieval (the current revision plus all pending revisions). The reviewed set is unordered; duplicates are invalid. The coordinator identity is authenticated and recorded.
+
+The server recomputes the current and pending set. If the reviewed set does not exactly match that set, the resolution is rejected with a conflict and no state changes; the conflict response includes the recomputed current revision ID and reviewed set so the coordinator can refresh. If the selected revision is unknown, belongs to another visit, or is not in the reviewed set, the request is rejected as invalid. A resolution for a visit with no pending revisions is rejected as having no review work.
+
+On acceptance, the selected revision becomes current. Every other revision in the reviewed set that was current or pending becomes declined. The resolution outcome records the coordinator ID, the selected revision, the contributing revision IDs (the reviewed set), and the resolution time. Payloads are not modified.
+
+A resolution is an adjudication, not a merge: no fields are copied between revisions. Selecting the current revision declines the pending revisions; selecting a pending revision replaces the current revision as the visit's whole record.
+
+Repeating a resolution key with an identical request returns the original resolution outcome; reuse with a different request is rejected. This distinguishes a retry from a stale review. Submissions accepted after a resolution follow the same rules. A revision based on the new current revision becomes current; any other revision becomes pending and makes the visit unresolved again.
+
+## Downstream Contract Obligations
+
+Tablet-sync retains each visit's current revision ID and submits it as the base revision ID. It treats a `current` response as the new capture base and a `pending` response as awaiting coordinator review. On an idempotent replay it uses the original acceptance result only as confirmation that the submission was stored, then refreshes current state rather than adopting the replayed status as current.
+
+Review-export retrieves unresolved work, compares whole revisions using revision history, and resolves by selecting one revision and echoing the reviewed set. It uses a resolution idempotency key so a retry after a lost response does not look like a stale review. It holds unresolved visits out of the partner CSV and refreshes on a resolution conflict. It does not merge fields.
+
+Neither downstream node may invent its own concurrency semantics.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Concurrency policy | Whole-visit revisions ([decision doc](decisions/whole-visit-revisions.md)) | Field-aware three-way merge | Preserves each submitted visit as a coherent scientific record. Field-level merging can combine edits that are independently plausible but scientifically related, and would require maintained merge rules for observer lists and species entries. A larger review queue is accepted in exchange for not combining edits automatically. |
+| Revision identity | Server-assigned revision IDs, client-supplied base revision ID | Client-generated revision IDs; per-visit integer versions | Server assignment avoids collisions and lets the store guarantee uniqueness. The base ID gives an exact starting point without trusting client clocks or counters. |
+| Plot identity | Plot reference fixed at first revision | Allow a revision to change the plot; derive plot from current revision only | A visit is a record of one plot. Fixing the reference prevents a revision from silently re-assigning observations to a different plot. |
+| Conflict detection | A revision is pending when its base is not the current revision | Field-level overlap detection; vector clocks | Whole-visit semantics make any edit that missed the current revision a candidate for review, including edits to unrelated fields. This is the accepted cost of the policy and avoids inferring field relationships. |
+| Acceptance ordering | Serialize acceptance per visit and order history by acceptance sequence | Order by client time; order by receipt timestamp alone | A per-visit sequence gives deterministic history and a single current revision even when submissions arrive together. Arrival order determines only the provisional current revision, not scientific authority. |
+| Resolution granularity | Resolve the whole visit by selecting one revision and declining all other pending revisions | Resolve each fork separately; field-level merge resolution | One adjudication leaves a single current record and no residual pending set. Per-fork resolution would let a visit remain unresolved across multiple decisions and complicate export eligibility. |
+| Resolution conflict handling | Require the exact reviewed-revision set and a resolution idempotency key; reject stale resolutions | Last write wins; version token only | Prevents a coordinator from declining a revision they never saw when a new submission arrives during review, while letting a lost-response retry return the original outcome. |
+| Idempotency scope | Per authenticated author for submissions; per coordinator for resolutions | Global key namespace | A retry cannot collide with another worker's or coordinator's request. |
+| Declined revision retention | Retain all revisions, including declined ones | Delete declined submissions after resolution | The HLD requires preserving submitted edits and keeping the path from observation to export inspectable. Declined revisions remain part of the visit's history. |
+| Review authorization | Coordinators require the identity provider's coordinator role for history, unresolved work, and resolution | Any authenticated worker may perform review operations | The HLD assigns review to coordinators. Role assignment stays with the existing identity provider. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Resolved
+
+1. ✅ Concurrent visit edits use whole-visit revisions rather than field-aware three-way merge.
+
+### Deferred
+
+1. Whether a resolution may carry a free-text note for coordinator context. The record-store contract does not require one; review-export can request it if its interface needs it.
+2. Retention duration for declined revisions beyond the current season's export cycle. Payloads remain until an archival policy is defined.
+3. Pagination and sort controls for revision history and unresolved-work retrieval as record volume grows.
